\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186417375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $D$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine ${D}^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcd8ebdd-840f-56b3-b01b-b55941f100d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186417379 #edb/ref 1074 "We write down $D$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $D$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[D\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix ${D}^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{D}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
